// The tail-`match` half of D3b — FIXED at the source level, still PINNED at
// the generated-C level, and the gap between those two sentences is the point
// of this file.
//
// WHAT WAS BROKEN AND IS NOW FIXED
// A `match` in tail position has exactly one arm form that can carry a value:
// `pattern => expression,`. (A block-bodied arm parses its statements with
// `parse_statement`, which demands a `;`, so `Circle => { 1 }` is a parse
// error — "Expected ';' after expression", measured.) That form built a
// one-statement `Stmt::Expr` body which nothing lowered, so measured on the
// previous commit:
//
//     fn area_code(s: Shape) -> i64 {
//         match s { Shape::Circle => 1, Shape::Square => 2 }
//     }
//
//   emitted `1;` / `2;` in the branches and `area_code(Green)` printed -16.
//
// The parser now lowers the arms of a function body's tail `match` the same way
// it lowers the branches of a tail `if`, and the values asserted below are
// correct. That is what this driver's transcript proves.
//
// WHAT IS STILL OPEN, AND WHY THIS IS `known_violation:area_code`
// Code generation lowers a `match` to an `if` / `else if` chain with NO final
// `else` (src/codegen/mod.rs — visible in build_output/stdlib_tail_match.c):
//
//     if (_match_expr.tag == __Shape__Circle) { return 1; }
//     else if (_match_expr.tag == __Shape__Square) { return 2; }
//     else if (_match_expr.tag == __Shape__Triangle) { return 3; }
//
// so a tag matching no arm falls off the end. Nothing in this file can produce
// such a tag, but the emitted C does not carry that proof, and neither
// scripts/check-c-returns.py nor gcc will take it on trust — gcc says
// "non-void function does not return a value in all control paths
// [-Wreturn-type]" on exactly this function.
//
// That is a SEPARATE open defect from D3b: exhaustive `match` lowering (the
// last arm, or an exhaustiveness proof, should emit a plain `else`). Declaring
// this file `clean` would require weakening the invariant to make it pass,
// which is the one thing the gate exists to forbid. So it is pinned to the one
// function it corrupts, and the pin will fail loudly — as XPASS — the day
// codegen closes the gap.

enum Shape {
    Circle,
    Square,
    Triangle,
}

// The D3b shape for `match`. Every arm is a `pattern => expression` tail.
fn area_code(s: Shape) -> i64 {
    match s {
        Shape::Circle => 1,
        Shape::Square => 2,
        Shape::Triangle => 3,
    }
}

// Block-bodied arms with explicit returns were never part of D3b — they emitted
// `return` before this change and must still. Their C has the same missing
// final `else`, so this function is pinned too if it is ever added to the
// violation set; today it is NOT, because DRIVERS.tsv names `area_code` only
// and the gate requires the sets to match exactly. Keeping this function here
// is what makes that a real check rather than a tautology.
fn sides(s: Shape) -> i64 {
    match s {
        Shape::Circle => {
            return 0;
        }
        Shape::Square => {
            return 4;
        }
        Shape::Triangle => {
            return 3;
        }
    }
}

fn expect_i64(label: String, actual: i64, expected: i64) {
    print(label);
    print_int(actual);
    if actual != expected {
        panic("tail-match: value mismatch");
    }
}

fn main() {
    expect_i64("area_circle", area_code(Shape::Circle), 1);
    expect_i64("area_square", area_code(Shape::Square), 2);
    expect_i64("area_triangle", area_code(Shape::Triangle), 3);
    expect_i64("sides_circle", sides(Shape::Circle), 0);
    expect_i64("sides_square", sides(Shape::Square), 4);
    expect_i64("sides_triangle", sides(Shape::Triangle), 3);
    print("tail_match_ok");
}
